  "notes": "The harder, tech-leaning third of the trio, and the entry states the trade explicitly: his documented range spans \"house, progressive house, tech house and electro house\", so \"the harmonic content is thinner and the rhythmic content denser than his partners'\". That is the encoded difference — two-chord families or a single sustained pad, a `maxChords` of 3, and against that the densest perc bed and the highest hat fill density of the three. The kit is his: \"harder, drier kick with a tighter clap; tech-house-derived percussion loops and shakers under the four-on-the-floor; longer, more repetitive phrase lengths (16 bars)\", which is a clap offset pinned at 0-2 ms, a shaker/perc/rim/ride bed at 4-7 hits a bar and 16-bar phrasing everywhere. The arrangement follows: \"groove-led build with percussion added rather than melody; a shorter breakdown; a rhythmic rather than melodic drop\", so the melody is riff-and-stab rather than an anthem. Bass is \"a driving sidechained saw or rubbery filtered bass carrying more of the groove than his partners' productions do\" — the lowest root probability of the three. ⚠ The research marks the harmonic reading `[LOW-CONF]` and calls the parameters convention-derived; both marks are repeated here, and all numbers are INFERRED. ⚠ **`chords.harmonicRhythm` re-weighted (2026-08-17, harmony variety floor).** The entry's harmony line is \"minor-mode vamps, often reduced to two chords or a single sustained pad\", and the engine's `vamp` value is *one* chord held for the whole clip (`generators::chords`: \"One chord for the whole pattern\") — so weighting `vamp` at the top rendered a **one**-chord bed the research nowhere describes. The chord motion the entry states is now carried by `1_per_bar` (the pair over a 2-bar cell) and `2_bars_per_chord` (the pair over a 4-bar cell), with `vamp` kept as the minority for the held reading. ⚠ The split between those two rates is **INFERRED** — the entry names the device, not its rate.",
